From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SheepyLord <37598021+SheepyLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 01:17:07 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Atmosphere: the Moon by day

The moons were drawn at night only: each disc faded in as the sun set
(smoothstep(0, -0.09, sun.y)) and then replaced the background where it
covered. A first-quarter Moon stood in the afternoon sky and was not there.

A moon lies beyond the air. Its light, already dimmed by the air in front
of it (moonAirTrans), is now ADDED to the sky's in-scatter, which the disc
no longer hides; its coverage hides only what lies beyond it: the stars and
the night sky image, the airglow layer and the halos as the sky treats
them, the moons before it in array order, and the everyday sun disc. By
day the blue shows through the dark side and over a pale lit side. With
the sky's in-scatter faded out (atmosphereFade, sun 16 degrees down) the
sum is exactly the old replace composite, so the night is unchanged; above
that the twilight's own light now lies over the disc instead of under it.

By day a moon's light follows the real Moon's phase law. evalTexturedMoon's
Lunar-Lambert keeps partial phases far brighter than the real Moon (a
quarter at 37% of full against 9%, a crescent at 13% against 1.9%), which
the night hides above the exposure's white point but which by day made a
crescent a white sticker one to three times brighter than the sky.
rtx_moon_daylight.h scales each moon's light while the sun is up by the
real law (Allen: 0.026 a + 4e-9 a^4 magnitudes) over the drawn one (the
Lommel-Seeliger and Lambert disc integrals), held from 150 degrees, times
rtx.atmosphere.daytimeMoonBrightness (float, default 1, 0..4; 0 = no moon by
day). The CPU folds it into four new floats, moonDaytimeScale0-3, one new
16-byte row of AtmosphereArgs that normalizeForSkyLutCache zeroes. The old
twilight band (sun 0 to 5 degrees down) now hands the light over to the
night's look; the halo and the clouds' moonlight stay night-only.

The horizon hides a disc below it (two pixels soft), as it hides the
stars: a moon below the horizon used to show through the ground's haze.
Without a horizon (rtx.atmosphere.noHorizon) the moons show where they are.
An eclipse hands the everyday sun disc over to its own before the Moon
touches it; with eclipses off the Moon's disc now covers the sun's.

tests/rtx/unit/test_moon_daylight.cpp checks the phase laws against a
brute-force disc integral and the day's scale at the GMod companion's
phases (the GMod native loader project's gate compiles it from this patch).
The dev menu: Daytime Brightness under Moons, Global Lighting (the option's
description as its tooltip). Catalogs regenerated and translated;
RtxOptions.md.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
